/**
 * @page asan_page ASan
 * 
 * This file describes what ASan is and how to use it.
 * 
 * @section asan_intro_sec Introduction
 * 
 * AddressSanitizer (ASan) is a fast memory error detector.
 * 
 * @section setup_sec Setup
 * 
 * To use ASan, add the `-fsanitize=address` option to both the compiling and linking steps.
 * 
 * This will compile the program with AddressSanitizer enabled.
 * 
 * @section usage_sec Usage
 * 
 * When running a program that is compiled with ASan enabled, whenever there are invalid memory accesses, such as out-of-bounds reads and writes, invalid frees, double frees, uses after free, and so on, there is a message printed out into the terminal the program is running in.
 * Additionally, by default on Linux, it also enables <a href="https://clang.llvm.org/docs/LeakSanitizer.html">LeakSanitizer</a>, which checks for memory leaks at the end of the execution of the program, and prints any found leaks into the terminal.
 * 
 * @section limit_sec Limitations
 * 
 * <b></b>
 * 
 * @if RST_SUPPORT
 * @embedRstVerbatim{
 * .. admonition:: Warning
 * 
 *    Not a static analysis tool!}
 * @else
 * @warning Not a static analysis tool!
 * @endif
 * 
 * Since ASan is not a static analyzer, it can only detect issues in the code that is being executed.
 * That means any code paths that were not taken during the program execution are unchecked and may still contain problems.
 * 
 * @section asan_info_sec More information
 * 
 * <ul>
 *    <li> <a href="https://clang.llvm.org/docs/AddressSanitizer.html">AddressSanitizer</a> </li>
 *    <li> <a href="https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/AddressSanitizer">Google's AddressSanitizer wiki</a> </li>
 *    <li> GCC's <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Instrumentation-Options.html">Program Instrumentation Options</a> </li>
 * </ul>
 */